# A timeout that walks away from a child process leaves the child running (2026-10-04)

Two calls in the session code hand a `tokio::process::Command` future to
`tokio::time::timeout` and, when the budget expires, walk away from a `git` that is still
running. This log records what the dependency actually promises, what the tree contains, what
was changed, and what the new gate `scripts/ci/check-timeout-child.py` can and cannot see.
Everything below ran on the working tree at `a04e91a7` plus the changes described here; the
source mutations in section 4 are undone in section 6 and verified undone.

== 0. The premise, read out of tokio rather than out of memory

`Cargo.lock` pins tokio 1.52.3, and a fixture asserts that the version the gate's docstring
cites is the version the lockfile names. The six lines the argument rests on:

    $ grep -n 'paradigm of dropping-implies-cancellation\|this value is assumed to be\|The destructor of the future returned by this function will kill\|let child = self.spawn();' ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/tokio-1.52.3/src/process/mod.rs
        202://! paradigm of dropping-implies-cancellation, a spawned process will, by
        641:    /// By default, this value is assumed to be `false`, meaning the next spawned
        974:    /// The destructor of the future returned by this function will kill
        1003:        let child = self.spawn();
        1037:    /// The destructor of the future returned by this function will kill
        1069:        let child = self.spawn();

Line 202 is inside the module documentation at `:201-203`:

    Similar to the behavior to the standard library, and unlike the futures
    paradigm of dropping-implies-cancellation, a spawned process will, by
    default, continue to execute even after the `Child` handle has been dropped.

`:974` belongs to `pub fn status(&mut self) -> impl Future<...>` at `:1002` and `:1037` to
`pub fn output(&mut self) -> impl Future<...>` at `:1065`; the two `self.spawn()` lines are
inside those bodies, so the child exists before anything is awaited. That leaves one lever,
`kill_on_drop`, and `:641` says the default is `false`.

The lint that looks like it covers this cannot reach the shape, because the calls in question
never hand out the handle the lint's alternative needs:

    $ grep -n 'Command::spawn' clippy.toml
        46:    { path = "std::process::Command::spawn", reason = "an unenrolled child outlives its session; use xai_tty_utils::ProcessScope::enroll" },
        49:    { path = "tokio::process::Command::spawn", reason = "an unenrolled child outlives its session; use xai_tty_utils::ProcessScope::enroll", allow-invalid = true },

    $ grep -n 'pub fn enroll' crates/codegen/xai-tty-utils/src/process_scope.rs
        137:    pub fn enroll(&self, child: &tokio::process::Child) -> io::Result<Arc<ProcessGroup>> {
        158:    pub fn enroll_std(&self, child: &std::process::Child) -> io::Result<Arc<ProcessGroup>> {

`spawn()` is the one call in this family that returns a `Child` to enroll. `.output()` and
`.status()` return a future over the output, so a site can satisfy the ban and still orphan
its child. The gate checks the half the ban cannot reach, and its fixtures assert the two
signatures above so that the argument cannot rot quietly if `ProcessScope` ever grows a
handle-free variant.

