That is the tree after the fix. The same script run against `a04e91a7` itself, in a throwaway
clone of that commit with the two new files copied in, reported exactly the two sites that the
fix then marked:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-timeout-child.py
        FAIL timeout children: 2 unrecorded site(s), 0 ledger/config problem(s)
        crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/goal_classifier.rs:340: no-kill-on-drop: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/goal_classifier.rs::capture_git_baseline hands `cmd.output()` to a timeout, which drops the future while the child it spawned is still running; mark the command kill_on_drop(true) or record it in timeout-child-allowlist.tsv
        crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/workflow/host_service.rs:945: no-kill-on-drop: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/workflow/host_service.rs::git_diff_since hands `cmd.output()` to a timeout, which drops the future while the child it spawned is still running; mark the command kill_on_drop(true) or record it in timeout-child-allowlist.tsv

and exited 1. Both were the identical shape:

    // goal_classifier.rs, capture_git_baseline, GIT_BASELINE_CAPTURE_TIMEOUT = 1 s
    cmd.arg("rev-parse").arg("HEAD").current_dir(workspace_root);

    let output = match tokio::time::timeout(GIT_BASELINE_CAPTURE_TIMEOUT, cmd.output()).await {

    // workflow/host_service.rs, git_diff_since, DIFF_TIMEOUT = 20 s
        xai_tty_utils::detach_command(&mut cmd);

        let output = tokio::time::timeout(DIFF_TIMEOUT, cmd.output())
            .await

The second one is the worse of the two. `detach_command` installs a `pre_exec` hook that
`setsid`s the child into its own session (`xai-tty-utils/src/lib.rs:198`), so once the future
is dropped there is no process group left that the parent's teardown could signal; and unlike
`detach_search_command` at `:216`, which calls `cmd.kill_on_drop(true)` for exactly this
reason, it sets nothing.

The 10 already-correct sites are correct in three different ways, which is what the scanner
had to learn to read: 8 through the `git_command` builder at
`session/goal_classifier/evidence.rs:49`, 1 through `detach_search_command(&mut command)` in
`grep_files/tool.rs`, and 1 with the flag written inline in `auto_update.rs`.

== 2. The fix

    let mut cmd = tokio::process::Command::new(crate::util::subprocess::git_bin());
    cmd.arg("rev-parse")
        .arg("HEAD")
        .current_dir(workspace_root)
        // The budget below drops this future while `git` is still running, and a
        // spawned child survives that drop unless told otherwise.
        .kill_on_drop(true);

    let mut cmd = tokio::process::Command::new("git");
    cmd.arg("diff")
        .arg(commit)
        .current_dir(&self.params.cwd)
        .stdin(std::process::Stdio::null())
        .stdout(std::process::Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(std::process::Stdio::piped())
        // `detach_command` puts `git` in its own session, so nothing else can
        // reach it once the timeout below drops this future.
        .kill_on_drop(true)
        .envs(xai_tty_utils::pager_env());
    xai_tty_utils::detach_command(&mut cmd);

== 3. A test that observes the process, not the flag

A test that takes the timeout asserts on the timeout, so the fix above is exactly the kind of
change a green suite cannot distinguish from no change. The new test therefore points the
binary the shipped function resolves -- `GIT_BIN_PATH`, honoured by `util::subprocess::git_bin`
at `util/subprocess.rs:32` -- at a shell script that records its own pid and then blocks:

    #!/bin/sh
    echo $$ > '/tmp/.tmpXXXX/shim.pid'
    exec sleep 8

`capture_git_baseline` spawns that shim itself, the 1 s budget expires, and the test then
watches `/proc/<pid>/stat` until the pid is either gone or a zombie. State `Z` counts as dead:
the kill landed and only the runtime's background reap is outstanding.

The gate is `#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]`, not `cfg(unix)`, and the reason is honesty about
the observation. On macOS there is no `/proc`, `read_to_string` fails on the first iteration,
and the loop's `Err(_) => break` would let every assertion below pass for the wrong reason.
The test asserts `/proc/self/stat` is readable before it starts, so a host without `/proc`
fails loudly instead of vacuously; `kill_on_drop` itself is tokio's own code and behaves the
same on every unix.

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib session::goal_classifier::tests::baseline_capture_timeout_kills_the_git_it_abandoned -- --exact
        running 1 test
        test session::goal_classifier::tests::baseline_capture_timeout_kills_the_git_it_abandoned ... ok

        test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6893 filtered out; finished in 1.02s

1.02 s is the 1 s capture budget plus the loop's first look at the pid file.

== 4. The test is not decorative, and neither is the gate

The behavioural test, with `.kill_on_drop(true)` removed from `capture_git_baseline` and
nothing else changed:

    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib session::goal_classifier::tests::baseline_capture_timeout_kills_the_git_it_abandoned -- --exact

        thread 'session::goal_classifier::tests::baseline_capture_timeout_kills_the_git_it_abandoned' (82102) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/goal_classifier_tests.rs:3700:9:
        the abandoned git shim (pid 82103) was still in state S 6.006851092s after the capture budget expired
        test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6893 filtered out; finished in 6.01s
        RED-EXIT=101

State `S` is interruptible sleep, which is what `exec sleep 8` does for the remaining 5 s of
its life; 6.01 s is the capture budget plus the assertion's own 5 s deadline, so a red run
leaks nothing long-lived. The file was restored from a copy and compared with
`filecmp(shallow=False)`: byte-identical.

