M1 takes the flag out of the builder and lights up all 8 call sites that depend on it, which is
what proves the builder is read rather than the file containing the string `kill_on_drop`
somewhere. M3 writes the flag as `false` and is reported under its own category, so the
argument is read and not the token. M4 changes a helper in a different crate and moves the
finding to the site in `xai-grok-tools` that goes through it.

M5 is the ledger rule, run against this tree with a row whose finding is gone:

