== 5. What the fixtures pin, and what the gate learned from this tree

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-timeout-child.py
        Ran 36 tests in 47.071s

        OK

Each negative case sits next to a positive case of the same shape, so a scanner that stopped
matching cannot pass by reporting nothing. Four of the 36 run the gate on this repository, one
of them asserting that both sites this gate found are still marked. Three of the judgements
exist because the first version of the scanner was wrong about this tree, and each has its own
case now:

- `git_command` is defined in three crates here, two of them building a `std::process::Command`,
  so an unqualified name resolves inside the calling crate first. The case pairs two crates
  whose `git_command` disagree (only the broken crate is reported) with two definitions of
  `git_command` inside one crate (reported as ambiguous rather than resolved by whichever the
  scan reached last).
- `use std::process::Command as StdCommand;` inside a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` made a
  tokio-only file read as ambiguous. Aliases are now excluded and `{...}` use trees are parsed
  entry by entry.
- `timeout(d, dispatch(drained.output()))` in a test is a tool output, not a child. The
  discriminator is the compiler's own: `Command::output` takes `&mut self`, so a receiver that
  is not a `let mut` binding cannot be one.

== 6. The tree afterwards

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-timeout-child.py
        timeout children hold: 12 timeout-abandoned output/status site(s), 12 kill their child on drop, 0 are std commands, 0 recorded

    $ git status --porcelain | wc -l
        11

Eleven entries, all of them this batch's own work: eight modified files and three new ones. No
mutation residue survived, and `git diff --stat` taken before the first mutation and after the
last is identical.

== 7. What is deliberately not covered

Stated in the gate's docstring as well, because a gate that only records what it caught invites
the reader to assume the rest is fine.

- `kill_on_drop` signals the child's pid, not its process group. A `git` that spawns `grep`
  and then dies by the flag can leave the grandchild. Nothing here checks that; it is a
  different question with a different rule.
- A future wrapped before it reaches `timeout` (`timeout(d, run(cmd))`,
  `cmd.output().map(..)`) is reported as `unreadable-future` rather than judged, because what
  reaches the child cannot be read from the call site.
- A command that is not a plain local -- a field, a `&mut` parameter of another function --
  is `unreadable-receiver`.
- A helper whose body is not in the tree is `unknown-helper`. `xai_tty_utils::mystery(&mut cmd)`
  is a fixture, not a finding anyone has to live with: the fix is to mark the command or record
  the row.
- `std::process::Command` is counted separately and never reported, because its `.output()`
  blocks the calling thread until the child exits, so there is no future to walk away from.
- A command reaching `.output()` with no timeout at all is left alone, including one inside a
  helper that a `timeout` happens to call. Cancellation can still come from the surrounding
  task, but "somebody might drop this task" is not a locally checkable property, so the gate
  judges only the sites where the code itself schedules the drop.
